srd009-du, and a whole batch of utility commands like true, false, basename, dirname, and various text processing tools. There's some inconsistency though with testutils and a few others where the code_status doesn't quite align with what I'd expect.\n\nLet me think through what's actually in requirement_states versus what's marked as not started in the code_status. The ones not showing up in requirement_states might mean their requirements are already satisfied, or they weren't part of the original generation scope. But I'm seeing srd003-format and testutils marked as \"not started\" in the code_status while missing from requirement_states, which is a bit confusing.\n\nActually, I think requirement_states is the source of truth here—if something isn't listed there as ready, I shouldn't be proposing tasks for it regardless of what the code_status says. That older status might just be stale from a previous run.\n\nSo I need to focus on which SRDs actually have ready requirements in requirement_states, then map those back to their releases to find the earliest incomplete one I should be working on. I'm noticing a pattern here—the requirement_states list only includes SRDs that still have pending work, while completed or not-yet-started items are missing from it. Let me verify this by checking if srd024-expand (for the expand command) is actually absent from requirement_states, since srd025-unexpand is present but expand itself isn't listed. it's either fully complete or not yet ready.\n\nGiven the recent commits show unexpand work just completed (R1.1-R1.4), and R2.1-R4.4 remain ready, the next task should continue with srd025-unexpand.\n\nBut I also need to check: are there earlier releases with remaining work?\n\nLooking at the requirement_states list, the earliest release SRDs I see are:\n- srd025-unexpand (rel05.1) - has ready requirements R2.1-R4.4\n- srd026-cut (rel05.2)\n- srd004-ts (rel15.0)\n\nI don't see any SRDs from releases 00.0 through 05.0 in the requirement_states. This means either those are all complete or they haven't been started yet but aren't in the generation scope for this run.\n\nSince we're on generation-run44 working through releases sequentially, and the recent commits show we've finished expand (rel05.1) and are now on unexpand (rel05.1), the earliest incomplete release with ready requirements is srd025-unexpand with R2.1-R4.4 available. After that comes rel05.2 with cut and paste, then rel05.3 with uniq and comm. Now I need to check the weights since the requirement_states don't include them directly, but requirements.yaml should have weight fields for each requirement. I need to distribute these 10 requirements across tasks while respecting the 4-requirement and weight-4 limits per task. Task 1 can accommodate R2.1 through R2.3 with room for one more, Task 2 handles R3.1 through R3.3, and Task 3 takes all four R4 requirements at maximum weight. This keeps me within the 4-task constraint while organizing the work logically by requirement group. So I'd complete the fourth task with a contract for the cut utility. I need to verify there aren't any blocking dependencies—since the base unexpand implementation is already in place from R1, the subsequent requirement groups should stack on top of it. Looking at the roadmap details about unexpand's functionality, the requirements probably break down into core features, extended options, edge cases, and error handling across those four groups.\n\nBut I'm realizing I can't actually infer the full SRD structure without access to it. The project context only gives me requirement states, not the actual specification content. To properly scope these tasks, I'd need to reference the SRD directly in the required_reading so the stitch agent can pull that information.\n\nLooking at what I do know: R1.1-R1.4 are complete from the recent commits, and I have requirement states showing R2-R4 are ready for srd025-unexpand. The roadmap describes the overall functionality—expand and unexpand as inverse utilities with shared code, custom tab stops, and differential testing against GNU coreutils.\n\nI should structure the remaining tasks by requirement group, starting with R2.1-R2.3 for the core unexpand implementation. I'm verifying the weight allocations for each task to ensure they stay within the budget limit of 4 points per task. The first three tasks for unexpand come in at 3 points each, and the fourth task for cut reaches exactly 4 points with its four requirements.\n\nNow I need to structure the task descriptions so they're self-contained for the stitch agent—each one should specify the SRD file path, which files to create or modify, the specific requirements being addressed, design decisions, and acceptance criteria. For the unexpand tasks, I'll be modifying the main implementation file and the test file, though I'm reconsidering whether the constraint about splitting implementation and tests into separate tasks applies here given how the SRD requirements are already grouped. within each requirement. The constraint about splitting impl/test is in P2, but P6 says \"Each SRD requirement maps to exactly one task.\" Let me re-read...\n\nP2 says: \"Always split implementation and tests into separate tasks. Decompose each feature into up to three tasks in this order: (1) types and interfaces, (2) implementation, (3) tests.\"\n\nBut P6 says: \"Anchor each task to specific SRD requirements by ID... Each SRD requirement maps to exactly one task unless the requirement exceeds the line budget, in which case split into implementation and test tasks that both reference the same requirement.\"\n\nSo the question is whether the tests are part of the requirement or separate. In this project's pattern, each cmd/ directory has main.go and <name>_test.go. The differential tests are part of the implementation verification.\n\nLooking at the existing commit pattern: \"Task 5273: [stitch] cmd/unexpand: implement srd025-unexpand R1.1-R1.4\" - this seems to be a combined implementation+test task.\n\nGiven the project's structure where utilities have main.go and *_test.go colocated, and differential testing is integral to verification, I think combining implementation and testing in the same task makes sense for each requirement group. The SRD requirements themselves likely include test expectations.\n\nFor line counts, a utility like unexpand probably totals 200-400 lines of main.go plus 100-200 lines of tests, so splitting by requirement groups keeps things reasonable. Since R1.1-R1.4 already created the base implementation, R2.x, R3.x, R4.x are modifying existing files rather than creating new ones.\n\nNow I need to be more specific about what each requirement group actually covers before writing the task descriptions. reading the SRD, I should at least reference them by ID and let the stitch agent read the SRD.\n\nFor the task titles, I'll follow the pattern from the recent commits: \"[stitch] cmd/unexpand: implement srd025-unexpand R2.1-R2.3\"\n\nLet me also think about the contract task for cut. From srd026-cut, the requirements are:\nR1.1, R1.2, R1.3, R1.4 (group 1)\nR2.1, R2.2, R2.3, R2.4 (group 2)\nR3.1, R3.2, R3.3 (group 3)\nR4.1, R4.2, R4.3, R4.4 (group 4)\n\nA contract task defines exported types, interfaces, and function signatures as Go stubs. It would create cmd/cut/main.go with stubs.\n\nBut I need to respect the constraint that contract tasks must come before any implementation tasks for a new package. With a 4-task limit, I should propose the unexpand implementation tasks first, then the cut contract task as my fourth slot: unexpand R2.1-R2.3, unexpand R3.1-R3.3, unexpand R4.1-R4.4, and finally the cut contract task with dependency -1. I'm setting up the task dependencies now—R2 features depend on the base, R3 builds on R2, and R4 handles error cases on top of that, all sequential since they're modifying the same file. The cut task runs independently. I need to reference the correct SRD file pa